### TL;DR  
`map.setFog()` is a **Mapbox-GL-JS-only** method.  
In MapLibre GL JS you get the same “haze-at-the-horizon” effect by adding a **sky object** (or calling `map.setSky()`) and configuring its fog-related properties.  
The fog only appears when 3-D terrain is active (or the map is pitched) – otherwise the sky is just a solid background colour.

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste ready snippet that reproduces the classic Mapbox `setFog` call with MapLibre-compatible code.

---

## 1. What changed?

| Mapbox GL JS (v2) | MapLibre GL JS (v2.x / v3.x) |
|-------------------|------------------------------|
| `map.setFog({ "color": "...", "highColor": "...", "horizonBlend": 0.5, "range": [0.8, 8] })` | **No `setFog` method**. Fog is part of the **sky** object. |
| Fog works even without terrain (it just blends into the background). | Fog is only rendered when **terrain is enabled** (or the map is pitched) because it is drawn as part of the atmospheric scattering that lives on the 3-D globe. |
| `sky` layer type does **not** exist. | `sky` is a **root-level style object** (or set via `map.setSky()`). |

So the migration steps are:

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** (if you don’t already have one) – terrain is required for fog.  
2. **Enable terrain** with `map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1 })`.  
3. **Add a sky object** (or call `map.setSky()`) and set the fog-related keys.  

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Horizon haze (fog) replacement</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base raster imagery (replace with your own)
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        },
        // 2️⃣  DEM source – required for fog
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/tiles.json',
          tileSize: 256,
          // encoding must match the source (here it is “mapbox”)
          // If you use Terrarium or other encodings, set `encoding: 'terrarium'`
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'sat', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 60,          // 👈 pitch is needed to see the sky/fog
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true    // required for 3-D terrain rendering
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Enable terrain (must be done before sky/fog)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add the sky (this is where fog lives)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      // Sky colour – you can keep the Mapbox default or change it
      'sky-color': '#88ccee',          // light blue
      // Horizon colour – blends into the sky colour
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      // How much the horizon colour blends into the sky colour (0-1)
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

      // ----------- Fog (haze) settings -------------
      // Colour of the fog that appears near the ground
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',          // a soft, slightly desaturated blue
      // How much the fog colour blends into the horizon colour (0-1)
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      // How much the fog colour blends into the ground (0-1)
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
      // Optional: you can also set `atmosphere-blend` (0-1) to control overall atmospheric density
      'atmosphere-blend': 0.25
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Add a hillshade or color-relief layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example hillshade – not required for fog, but often used together
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Normal MapLibre `new maplibregl.Map`. The style can be an inline object (as above) or a URL to a style JSON. |
| **2️⃣ DEM source** | `type: 'raster-dem'`. This is the only source that can feed terrain, hillshade, color-relief, and the sky-fog. |
| **3️⃣ Enable terrain** | `map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 })`. Fog will not render until terrain is on **and** the map is pitched. |
| **4️⃣ Add the sky** | `map.setSky({ … })` replaces the old `map.setFog`. The three fog-related keys (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) give you the same “haze at the horizon” effect. |
| **5️⃣ (Optional) Hillshade** | Shows how you can keep the classic hillshade layer alongside the sky/fog. |

---

## 3. Mapping the old `setFog` options to the new sky properties

| Mapbox `setFog` option | MapLibre sky property | Effect |
|------------------------|-----------------------|--------|
| `color` (the fog colour) | `fog-color` | Colour of the haze that appears near the ground. |
| `highColor` (sky colour) | `sky-color` | Overall sky colour (the “high” part of the gradient). |
| `horizonBlend` (0-1) | `sky-horizon-blend` **or** `horizon-fog-blend` | Controls how the horizon colour blends into the sky colour (`sky-horizon-blend`) and how the fog colour blends into the horizon (`horizon-fog-blend`). |
| `range` (e.g. `[0.8, 8]`) | Not directly exposed. The fog density is controlled by the three blend factors (`sky-horizon-blend`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) and by the camera pitch. You can approximate a “far-away” fog by lowering the blend values. |
| `spaceColor` (Mapbox-GL-JS 2.6+) | `sky-color` (same) | Same as `highColor`. |

If you used a custom `spaceColor` or `highColor` that is a gradient, you’ll need to approximate it with a single colour plus the blend values – MapLibre does not support a full colour ramp for the sky.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to troubleshoot

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No haze at all, even after `setSky`** | Terrain not enabled **or** map pitch is `0`. | Call `map.setTerrain(...)` **and** set `pitch` > 0 (e.g. `map.easeTo({ pitch: 60 })`). |
| **Fog looks too strong / completely white** | `fog-color` is too bright or blend values are `1`. | Use a slightly desaturated, semi-transparent colour (e.g. `rgba(200,210,255,0.8)`) and lower the blend factors (`0.3-0.6`). |
| **Fog disappears when zooming out** | At very low zoom the terrain mesh is too coarse, so the sky is rendered behind it. | Increase the DEM maxzoom (or use higher-resolution DEM tiles) or keep the map at a moderate zoom when you need the haze. |
| **Console shows “unknown property: fog-color”** | Using a MapLibre version **older than 2.4** (the sky object was added in v2.4). | Upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS (≥ 2.4, preferably the current 4.x). |
| **`map.setSky` is undefined** | You are using a very old MapLibre version (< 2.4) or you imported the *lite* build. | Use the full build (`maplibre-gl.js`) from the CDN or npm package, not the `maplibre-gl-lite` bundle. |
| **Fog works but the sky colour is wrong** | You set `sky-color` but also have a `background` layer that overwrites it. | Remove or place the `background` layer **below** the sky (or set its colour to `transparent`). |

---

## 5. Quick “one-liner” migration cheat-sheet

If you have a Mapbox call that looks like this:

```js
map.setFog({
  color: 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)',   // fog colour
  highColor: '#88ccee',            // sky colour
  horizonBlend: 0.5,
  range: [0.8, 8]                  // ignored in MapLibre
});
```

Replace it with:

```js
// 1️⃣ Make sure terrain is on (required for fog)
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

// 2️⃣ Add the sky (fog lives inside the sky object)
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#88ccee',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',          // optional – defaults to sky-color if omitted
  'fog-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)', // same as Mapbox `color`
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

That’s it – the visual result should be indistinguishable from the original Mapbox implementation (provided you have terrain enabled and a non-zero pitch).

---

