### Bottom line

*Replace `map.setFog()` with a sky object (`map.setSky()`), make sure a `raster-dem` source is present, enable terrain, and pitch the map.* Once those three conditions are met you’ll get the same atmospheric haze that you were used to in Mapbox GL JS. Happy mapping!

### TL;DR  
`map.setFog()` is a **Mapbox-only** API.  
In MapLibre GL JS you get “haze” (fog) from the **sky** object, not from a fog layer.  
Add a sky (or put a `sky` object in your style) and set the three fog-related properties:

| Property | What it does | Typical value |
|----------|--------------|---------------|
| `fog-color` | colour of the atmospheric haze (blends with the sky) | `"#c0d8ff"` |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | how quickly the fog fades into the horizon line | `0.5` |
| `fog-ground-blend` | how much fog reaches the ground (needs 3-D terrain) | `0.5` |

You also need **3-D terrain** (or at least a pitched view) for the fog to be visible – a flat, top-down map will never show it.

---

## 1. Make sure you have a `raster-dem` source and terrain turned on  

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the DEM source (any raster-dem that matches your imagery)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // optional: encoding: 'terrarium' | 'mapbox' (default is mapbox)
  maxzoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣ Enable terrain (exaggeration is optional)
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
```

> **Why?**  
> Fog is part of the *sky* object and is only rendered when the map is in 3-D mode (i.e. `pitch > 0` and terrain is present). If you keep `pitch: 0` you’ll just see the background colour.

---

## 2. Add a sky (the replacement for `setFog`)

### Option A – **Programmatic** (recommended if you want to toggle fog at runtime)

```js
// Make sure the map is already loaded
map.on('load', () => {
  // 3️⃣ Add the sky
  map.setSky({
    // Sky colour – you can keep the default or choose your own
    'sky-color': '#199EF3',          // light blue
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',      // bright horizon
    // Fog (haze) settings
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',          // a soft blue-gray haze
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,        // how quickly fog meets the horizon
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,         // how far fog reaches the ground
    // Optional extra blending controls (available from GL JS 5.20+)
    // 'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    // 'atmosphere-blend': 0.5,
  });

  // 4️⃣ Tilt the camera so you can see the effect
  map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, bearing: 0, duration: 2000 });
});
```

### Option B – **Style-based** (if you prefer to keep everything in the JSON style)

Add a top-level `sky` object to your style JSON (or to the style you load with `map.setStyle()`):

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { /* … your sources … */ },
  "layers": [ /* … your layers … */ ],
  "terrain": {
    "source": "terrain-dem",
    "exaggeration": 1
  },
  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#199EF3",
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "fog-color": "#c0d8ff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.5,
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5
  }
}
```

> **Note:** The `sky` object lives **outside** the `layers` array – it is a root-level property, just like `terrain`.

---

## 3. Verify the result

1. **Pitch the map** (e.g. `map.easeTo({ pitch: 60 })`).  
2. You should see a blue-gray haze that gradually blends into the sky near the horizon.  
3. If the haze is still missing:
   * Confirm that `map.getTerrain()` returns a non-null object.  
   * Make sure you are using a recent MapLibre GL JS version (≥ 5.5 for `setSky`, ≥ 5.6 for `color-relief`, but fog works from the first `sky` implementation in 5.5).  
   * Check the console for warnings like “`sky` object ignored – terrain not enabled” – that usually means terrain is missing or the map is still at `pitch: 0`.

---

## 4. Quick checklist (copy-paste)

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Load MapLibre
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// 2️⃣  Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // or your own style
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 4,
  pitch: 0,          // start flat, we'll tilt later
  antialias: true   // needed for 3-D terrain
});

// 3️⃣  When the style is ready, add DEM & terrain
map.on('load', () => {
  map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
    maxzoom: 12
  });

  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

  // 4️⃣  Add sky (fog)
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#199EF3',
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
  });

  // 5️⃣  Tilt the view so fog becomes visible
  map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, duration: 1500 });
});
```

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No haze at all** | `pitch` is still `0` or terrain not set | Call `map.setTerrain()` *and* tilt the map (`pitch > 0`). |
| **Fog appears but is pure white** | `fog-color` left at default (`#fff`) or sky-color overrides it | Choose a semi-transparent blue/gray (`rgba(200,200,255,0.5)` or hex `#c0d8ff`). |
| **Console says “unknown property: fog-color”** | Using an old MapLibre version (< 5.5) | Upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS (≥ 5.5). |
| **Fog works but disappears when you zoom out** | `fog-ground-blend` is too low, so fog only covers near-ground tiles | Increase `fog-ground-blend` (e.g., `0.7`). |
| **`map.setSky` throws “is not a function”** | You are still on Mapbox GL JS or an old MapLibre build that predates sky support | Use a recent MapLibre bundle (`npm i maplibre-gl@latest`). |

---

## 6. Bonus: Adding a subtle colour-relief layer (optional)

If you also want the terrain to be tinted by elevation (a hypsometric map) **and** keep the fog, you can add a `color-relief` layer on top of the DEM:

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'elevation-tint',
  type: 'color-relief',
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  paint: {
    'color-relief-color': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['elevation'],
      0,   '#2c7bb6',
      500, '#abd9e9',
      1500,'#ffffbf',
      2500,'#fdae61',
      4000,'#ffffff'
    ],
    'color-relief-opacity': 0.6
  }
});
```

The fog will still be applied because it belongs to the sky, not to any particular layer.

---

## 7. TL;DR code snippet (the smallest thing that works)

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  });
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem' });

  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#199EF3',
    'horizon-color': '#fff',
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
  });

  map.easeTo({ pitch: 60 });
});
```

That’s it – you’ve replaced the old `map.setFog()` call with MapLibre’s sky-based fog, and you’ll see the same atmospheric haze at the horizon as you did in Mapbox GL JS. Happy mapping!
